Options II, Honour School of Mathematics Part A, Trinity Term; Thursday 24 June 2010, 9.30am–12.30pm. The complete fifteen-page source was supplied through https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/maths_past_papers_3[the past-papers disclosure], in original Part A bundle pages 213–227. The private editable transcription retains the complete cover and every printed question.

New solutions cover the eighteen questions without an exact worked-answer advertisement found in the bounded check. H2 is recorded separately because Oxford explicitly advertises its solutions for Oxford students in https://users.ox.ac.uk/~math0391/CMrevision.pdf[the Classical Mechanics revision notes]. No new solution is supplied for that question.

= A
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= A2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}
{c}

= 1
{parent=2010/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/A/A2/1}

The extension $K/F$ is a <vector space> over $F$. Since $K$ is finite, a maximal linearly independent subset is finite and spans it; let its size be $d$. Relative to this <basis>, every element of $K$ has a unique list of $d$ coefficients in $F$. There are $q$ choices for each coefficient, so
$$
\boxed{|K|=q^d,\qquad d=[K:F].}
$$
The inclusion $F\subseteq K$ makes $d\geq1$.

= 2
{parent=2010/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/A/A2/2}

Write $Q=q^m$ and take a <splitting field> $L$ of $X^Q-X$ over $F$. Its <formal derivative> is $-1$, since the characteristic divides $Q$, so it has $Q$ distinct <root>[roots] in $L$. Let $K$ be their set. The <Frobenius endomorphism> identity gives
$$
(u+v)^Q=u^Q+v^Q,\qquad(uv)^Q=u^Qv^Q.
$$
Thus $K$ contains $0,1$ and is closed under addition, negatives and multiplication. If $u\neq0$ belongs to $K$, then $(u^{-1})^Q=(u^Q)^{-1}=u^{-1}$, so it is closed under inverses too. Hence $K$ is a <field> with exactly $Q$ elements. Every $c\in F$ satisfies $c^q=c$ by <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups> applied to $F^\times$, together with the zero case; iterating gives $c^Q=c$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F\subseteq K,\qquad |K|=q^m.}
$$

= 3
{parent=2010/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/A/A2/3}

By the allowed finite multiplicative-<subgroup> theorem, the <group> $K^\times$ is cyclic. Let $z$ generate it. Every nonzero element of $K$ is a power of $z$, hence belongs to $F(z)$; zero belongs as well. Since the reverse inclusion is automatic,
$$
\boxed{K=F(z).}
$$
Thus a finite extension of a <finite field> is a <simple field extension>.

= 4
{parent=2010/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/A/A2/4}

For $\alpha\neq0$, <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups> in $K^\times$, whose order is $q^m-1$, gives $\alpha^{q^m-1}=1$. The zero element satisfies the equation too, so every $\alpha\in K$ has $\alpha^{q^m}=\alpha$.

Now let $f\in F[X]$ be irreducible of degree $m$. The quotient $F[X]/(f)$ is a <field> with $q^m$ elements, by its <basis> $1,\overline X,\ldots,\overline X^{m-1}$. Its element $\overline X$ therefore satisfies $\overline X^{q^m}-\overline X=0$. This means precisely that $X^{q^m}-X$ lies in the ideal $(f)$, giving
$$
\boxed{f(X)\mid X^{q^m}-X.}
$$

= B
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Group Theory}

= B2
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/B/B2/a}

With $N\triangleleft G$, the internal <semidirect product> conditions are $G=NH$ and $N\cap H=\{1\}$. Every element then has a unique expression $nh$. The extra data needed to reconstruct the <group> are the <conjugation> action
$$
\phi:H\to\operatorname{Aut}(N),\qquad\phi_h(n)=hnh^{-1}.
$$
On the set $N\times H$, the external <semidirect product> $N\rtimes_\phi H$ has multiplication
$$
\boxed{(n,h)(n',h')=(n\phi_h(n'),hh').}
$$
The map $(n,h)\mapsto nh$ identifies it with $G$. A direct product results when this action is trivial.

For example, $S_3=A_3\rtimes\langle(12)\rangle\cong C_3\rtimes C_2$. The two <subgroup>[subgroups] intersect trivially and their orders multiply to six, so they generate $S_3$. <Conjugation> by the transposition inverts the three-cycle. This action is nontrivial, and $S_3$ is nonabelian whereas $C_3\times C_2$ is abelian, so this is not their direct product.

= b
{parent=2010/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/B/B2/b}

Use composition from right to left. Applying $(C,d)$ and then $(A,b)$ gives
$$
v\mapsto A(Cv+d)+b=ACv+(Ad+b),
$$
so
$$
\boxed{(A,b)(C,d)=(AC,Ad+b).}
$$
The product remains an affine transformation because $AC$ is invertible. Composition of functions is associative. The identity is $(I,0)$, and solving $w=Av+b$ gives the inverse
$$
\boxed{(A,b)^{-1}=(A^{-1},-A^{-1}b).}
$$
Thus these transformations form the <affine group>. The representation by $(A,b)$ is unique: evaluating at zero determines $b$, and then evaluating on the standard <basis> determines $A$.

= c
{parent=2010/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/B/B2/c}

Embed $\operatorname{GL}_n(F)$ by $A\mapsto(A,0)$. The multiplication and inverse formulas show that this is an injective <group homomorphism>, so its image $H$ is a <subgroup>. The translations form $N=\{(I,c):c\in F^n\}$, with $(I,c)(I,d)=(I,c+d)$ and inverse $(I,-c)$. Hence $N$ is isomorphic to the additive <group> of $F^n$.

For an arbitrary affine transformation,
$$
\boxed{(A,b)(I,c)(A,b)^{-1}=(I,Ac).}
$$
<Conjugation> therefore preserves $N$, proving that $N$ is normal in $G$.

= d
{parent=2010/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/B/B2/d}

Every affine transformation factors as
$$
(A,b)=(I,b)(A,0),
$$
so $G=NH$. A translation fixing zero has zero translation vector, so $H\cap N=\{(I,0)\}$. Together with normality from part (c), these are the internal <semidirect product> conditions. Thus
$$
\boxed{G\cong(F^n,+)\rtimes\operatorname{GL}_n(F).}
$$
The reconstruction data are the additive <group> $F^n$, the linear <group> $\operatorname{GL}_n(F)$, and its standard action $A:c\mapsto Ac$ on translations. In pair notation $(b,A)$, the product is $(b,A)(d,C)=(b+Ad,AC)$, equivalent to the formula in part (b).

= e
{parent=2010/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/B/B2/e}

There are $p^n$ possible translation vectors. An <invertible matrix> is specified by an ordered <basis> of $\mathbb F_p^n$. Its first column can be any nonzero vector, giving $p^n-1$ choices. After choosing $i$ independent columns, their span has $p^i$ elements, so the next column has $p^n-p^i$ choices. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|G|=p^n|\operatorname{GL}_n(\mathbb F_p)|
=p^n\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(p^n-p^i).}
$$

= C
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Number Theory}

= C2
{parent=2010/C}
{scope}
{c}

= i
{parent=2010/C/C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/C/C2/i}

A <primitive root modulo n>[primitive root modulo $p$] is a <residue> class generating the cyclic multiplicative <group> $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$, so its order is $p-1$. If $g$ is one generator, all generators are $g^j$ with $\gcd(j,p-1)=1$. Thus the number is
$$
\boxed{\varphi(p-1),}
$$
where $\varphi$ is <Euler's totient function>. This includes $p=2$, with $\varphi(1)=1$.

= ii
{parent=2010/C/C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/C/C2/ii}

For $n\geq1$, the zero <residue> contributes zero. Write $S=\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p^\times}x^n$. Multiplication by any nonzero $\alpha$ permutes these residues, so $S=\alpha^nS$ in $\mathbb F_p$. If $(p-1)\nmid n$, choose a <primitive root> $g$; then $g^n\neq1$, and $(1-g^n)S=0$ forces $S=0$.

If $(p-1)\mid n$, every nonzero $x$ has $x^n=1$, so $S=p-1\equiv-1\pmod p$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{x=1}^p x^n\equiv
\begin{cases}0,&(p-1)\nmid n,\\-1,&(p-1)\mid n\end{cases}\pmod p\quad(n\geq1).}
$$
The source also lists $n=0$, although its premise assumes $n\geq1$. For that separate case, the <integer> sum has $p$ terms each equal to one, so it is zero modulo $p$.

= iii
{parent=2010/C/C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/C/C2/iii}

The <Legendre symbol> equal to $1$ says that $a$ is a nonzero square modulo $p$. Choose one <root> $r\neq0$. Then $-r$ is another <root>, distinct because $p$ is odd. Any solution $x$ satisfies
$$
0=x^2-r^2=(x-r)(x+r)
$$
in the <field> $\mathbb F_p$, so $x=r$ or $x=-r$. Thus there are
$$
\boxed{2\text{ solutions}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=2010/C/C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/C/C2/iv}

Let $R$ be the sum of the nonzero <quadratic residue>[quadratic residues]. Each has two square <root>[roots] by part (iii), so
$$
2R\equiv\sum_{x=1}^{p-1}x^2\equiv0\pmod p,
$$
where part (ii) applies because $p>3$ makes $p-1$ not divide $2$. Since $2$ is invertible modulo $p$, $R\equiv0$.

The sum of all nonzero classes is $p(p-1)/2\equiv0\pmod p$. Subtracting $R$ shows that the sum of <quadratic nonresidue>[quadratic nonresidues] is also zero. Consequently **both requested sums vanish modulo $p$**.

= v
{parent=2010/C/C2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/C/C2/v}

Part (ii), with exponent $30$, shows that a prime $q$ fails to divide the sum precisely when $q-1$ divides $30$. The positive divisors are $1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30$. Adding one gives $2,3,4,6,7,11,16,31$, of which the primes are
$$
\boxed{q\in\{2,3,7,11,31\}.}
$$
For these primes the sum is $-1$ modulo $q$; for every other prime it is zero.

= D
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Integration}

= D3
{parent=2010/D}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D3}

The <monotone convergence theorem> states that for nonnegative measurable functions $f_n\uparrow f$ almost everywhere, $\int f=\lim_n\int f_n$, allowing the value $+\infty$.

The <dominated convergence theorem> states that if measurable functions $f_n\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\leq g$ almost everywhere for some integrable $g$, then $f$ is integrable and $\int f_n\to\int f$. In fact $\int|f_n-f|\to0$ as well. Both statements apply on any <measure space>.

= 1
{parent=2010/D/D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D3/1}

Apply the <monotone convergence theorem> to $s_N=\sum_{k=1}^N|a_k|$. Its limit $s$ satisfies
$$
\int s=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\int|a_k|<\infty.
$$
Thus $s$ is finite almost everywhere, which proves <absolute convergence> of $\sum_k a_k$ almost everywhere. Define its sum $a$ there, and set $a=0$ on the exceptional <null set>. Then $|a|\leq s$, so $a$ is integrable. The partial sums of $\sum_k a_k$ are also dominated by $s$; <dominated convergence theorem>[dominated convergence] gives
$$
\boxed{\int\sum_{k=1}^\infty a_k=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^N\int a_k
=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\int a_k.}
$$
The series of integrals converges absolutely, since $|\int a_k|\leq\int|a_k|$.

= 2
{parent=2010/D/D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D3/2}

For $0<x<1$, expand $(1-x)^{-2}=\sum_{k\geq1}kx^{k-1}$. Multiplying by $(\log x)^2$ gives nonnegative terms, so the <monotone convergence theorem> permits termwise integration. Put $x=e^{-t}$ to obtain
$$
\int_0^1x^{k-1}(\log x)^2\,dx
=\int_0^\infty t^2e^{-kt}\,dt=\frac2{k^3},
$$
where scaling followed by two integrations by parts evaluates the last integral. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\left(\frac{\log x}{1-x}\right)^2dx
=2\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac1{k^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{3}.}
$$
The finite value also establishes integrability at both endpoints.

= D4
{parent=2010/D}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D4}

A sufficient <differentiation under the integral sign> theorem is the following local domination criterion. Suppose $u(t,\cdot)$ is measurable for each $t$, $u(t_0,\cdot)$ is integrable at one point of a neighborhood $J$ of $t_0$, and for almost every $x$ the function $u(\cdot,x)$ is differentiable on $J$. Assume an integrable function $g_J$ satisfies $|\partial_tu(t,x)|\leq g_J(x)$ for $t\in J$ and almost every $x$. Then $I$ is differentiable on the interior of $J$ and
$$
\boxed{I'(t)=\int_E\partial_tu(t,x)\,dx.}
$$
The <mean value theorem> bounds the difference quotients by $g_J$, and the <dominated convergence theorem> passes their pointwise limit through the integral. Applying this criterion locally throughout $(a,b)$ proves <differentiability> there. One may impose the stronger condition that every $u(t,\cdot)$ is integrable from the outset.

= 1
{parent=2010/D/D4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2010/D/D4/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D4/1/a}

For every <integer> $j\geq0$, the $j$th derivative with respect to $t$ of $\cos(t\cos x)$ is $(\cos x)^j$ times a sine or cosine with a sign. Its absolute value is at most $1$ on the finite <interval> $[0,\pi/2]$. This is an integrable dominating function independent of $t$. Repeated <differentiation under the integral sign> is therefore justified for every order, giving **$J_0\in C^\infty(\mathbb R)$**.

= b
{parent=2010/D/D4/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D4/1/b}

Differentiating the integral representation gives
$$
J_0'(t)=-\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos x\sin(t\cos x)\,dx,
\qquad
J_0''(t)+J_0(t)=\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^2x\cos(t\cos x)\,dx.
$$
Now integrate the derivative
$$
\frac d{dx}\big(\sin x\sin(t\cos x)\big)
=\cos x\sin(t\cos x)-t\sin^2x\cos(t\cos x).
$$
The boundary terms vanish at both endpoints. Consequently $J_0'(t)=-t(J_0''(t)+J_0(t))$, proving
$$
\boxed{tJ_0''(t)+J_0'(t)+tJ_0(t)=0.}
$$
The identity includes $t=0$, where it reads $J_0'(0)=0$.

= 2
{parent=2010/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/D/D4/2}

A naive absolute bound on the original derivative would be $1/x$, which is not integrable on $[1,\infty)$. Instead make the change of variable $s=tx$, valid for $t>0$:
$$
K(t)=t\int_t^\infty\frac{\cos s}{s^2}\,ds.
$$
The integral is absolutely convergent for every positive lower endpoint. Its integrand is continuous there, so the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_t^\infty\frac{\cos s}{s^2}\,ds=-\frac{\cos t}{t^2}.
$$
The product rule now yields
$$
\boxed{K'(t)=\int_t^\infty\frac{\cos s}{s^2}\,ds-\frac{\cos t}{t}
=\frac{K(t)-\cos t}{t}.}
$$
This proves <differentiability> and the equation without an unjustified differentiation of a conditionally convergent derivative integral.

= E
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Topology}

= E3
{parent=2010/E}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/E/E3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/E/E3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/a/i}

A subset $A$ is <connected space>[connected] if its <subspace topology> admits no separation into two disjoint nonempty relatively open subsets. Equivalently, every <continuous map> $A\to\{0,1\}$, with the target discrete, is constant: a nonconstant map gives the separation by its two fibers, and a separation defines such a map.

= ii
{parent=2010/E/E3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/a/ii}

The connected subsets of the usual real line are **the empty set and all <interval>[intervals]**, including singletons, bounded <interval>[intervals] of any endpoint type, rays, and the whole line. Equivalently, a nonempty subset is connected exactly when it contains every point between any two of its points.

= iii
{parent=2010/E/E3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/a/iii}

Use the characterization by continuous maps to the discrete two-point space. Let $A=\bigcup_iA_i$ and let $f:A\to\{0,1\}$ be continuous. Its restriction to each connected $A_i$ is constant. Since $A_i\cap A_j\neq\varnothing$, those two constants agree for every pair $i,j$. Hence $f$ is constant on their union. Thus **the union is connected**. If the indexing set is empty, the union is empty and is connected directly.

= b
{parent=2010/E/E3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/E/E3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/b/i}

Let
$$
\boxed{K_a=\bigcup\{S\subseteq X:S\text{ is connected and }a\in S\}.}
$$
This family is nonempty because it contains $\{a\}$. All its members intersect at $a$, so part (a)(iii) proves that $K_a$ is connected. It contains $a$ and, by its construction, contains every connected subset containing $a$. Thus it has exactly the required largest-subset property. This is the <connected component> containing $a$.

= ii
{parent=2010/E/E3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/b/ii}

Suppose two components $K$ and $L$ intersect. Their union is connected by part (a)(iii). It contains the point used to define $K$, so maximality forces $K\cup L\subseteq K$, giving $L\subseteq K$. The same reasoning with $L$ gives $K\subseteq L$. Hence $K=L$. Thus **distinct <connected component>[connected components] are disjoint**.

= iii
{parent=2010/E/E3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/b/iii}

Let $C$ be a component of the <open set> $X\subseteq\mathbb R$. Being a connected subset of the real line, $C$ is an <interval>. For any $x\in C$, openness of $X$ gives $\epsilon>0$ with $(x-\epsilon,x+\epsilon)\subseteq X$. This <interval> is connected and meets $C$, so their union is connected. Maximality of $C$ makes the entire <interval> lie in $C$. Thus every point of $C$ is interior, so **each component is an open <interval>**, allowing unbounded endpoints.

= iv
{parent=2010/E/E3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E3/b/iv}

Every point belongs to its component, so the components cover $X$; by part (ii) they are pairwise disjoint, and by part (iii) they are nonempty open <interval>[intervals]. Each contains a <rational number>. Fix an enumeration of $\mathbb Q$ and choose the first rational belonging to each component. Distinct components receive distinct rationals, giving an <injection> from the component family into a <countable set>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is a countable union of pairwise disjoint open intervals}.}
$$
The empty <open set> is represented by the empty union.

= E4
{parent=2010/E}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/E/E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/E/E4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/a/i}

A subset $K$ of a <topological space> is <compact space>[compact] if every cover of $K$ by open subsets of the ambient space has a finite subcover. This is equivalent to <compactness> of $K$ with its <subspace topology>.

= ii
{parent=2010/E/E4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/a/ii}

A subset $B$ of a <metric space> is <bounded set>[bounded] if it lies in some ball of finite radius: there are $x_0$ in the space and $R<\infty$ with $d(x,x_0)\leq R$ for every $x\in B$. For a nonempty subset, this is equivalent to finite <diameter>, $\sup_{x,y\in B}d(x,y)<\infty$. The empty subset is bounded by convention.

= iii
{parent=2010/E/E4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/a/iii}

In an arbitrary <topological space>, compact need not imply closed. In a two-point indiscrete space, a singleton is compact but not closed. Closed need not imply compact either: $\mathbb R$ is closed in itself but the cover $\{(-n,n):n\geq1\}$ has no finite subcover. One general theorem that does hold is that a closed subset $F$ of a <compact space> $K$ is compact: adjoin $K\setminus F$ to any relative open cover of $F$, take a finite cover of $K$, and discard that additional set.

In a <Hausdorff space>, every compact subset $K$ is closed. For $y\notin K$, separate $y$ from each $x\in K$ by disjoint <open set>[open sets] $V_x$ about $y$ and $U_x$ about $x$. Finitely many $U_x$ cover $K$; the intersection of their corresponding $V_x$ is a neighborhood of $y$ disjoint from $K$. Thus the complement of $K$ is open. The converse still fails, as the real-line example shows.

In every <metric space>, compact subsets are bounded. Cover a nonempty $K$ by unit balls centered on $K$ and take a finite subcover; the <triangle inequality> bounds all points relative to any one of the finitely many centers. The converse fails even for closed bounded subsets of general metric spaces. An infinite set with discrete metric $d(x,y)=1$ for distinct points is closed and bounded in itself, but its cover by singleton balls has no finite subcover. Thus **compact implies closed and bounded in a <metric space>, while closed and bounded need not imply compact**.

= iv
{parent=2010/E/E4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/a/iv}

By the <Heine-Borel theorem>, a subset of $\mathbb R$ is compact exactly when it is closed and bounded. Consequently the compact <interval>[intervals] are
$$
\boxed{[u,v]\quad\text{with finite }u\leq v,}
$$
including singleton <interval>[intervals]. The empty <interval> is compact too, if included in the <interval> convention. Nonempty open, proper half-open, and unbounded <interval>[intervals] are not compact.

= v
{parent=2010/E/E4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/a/v}

For any topological target $Y$, **$f(K)$ is compact**. Pull an open cover of $f(K)$ back by $f$, take a finite subcover of $K$, and map the covering statement forward. Without a separation assumption on $Y$, the image need not be closed, as a constant map into a nonclosed singleton of an indiscrete space shows.

If $Y$ is a <metric space>, $f(K)$ is also closed in $Y$, bounded, <totally bounded>, complete with its inherited metric, and sequentially compact. Closedness uses the Hausdorff theorem from part (iii); total boundedness follows by taking finite subcovers of the covers by balls of any fixed radius. Compact metric spaces are sequentially compact, and a <Cauchy sequence> with a convergent subsequence converges to the same limit, proving <completeness>. These properties are asserted for the image subset, without requiring the whole ambient <metric space> to be complete.

= b
{parent=2010/E/E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/E/E4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/b/i}

A sequence $(x_n)$ in a <metric space> is a <Cauchy sequence> if
$$
\boxed{\forall\epsilon>0\ \exists N\ \forall m,n\geq N: d(x_m,x_n)<\epsilon.}
$$
This compares sufficiently late terms with each other; it does not assume the existence of a limit in the space.

= ii
{parent=2010/E/E4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/b/ii}

A <metric space> is <complete metric space>[complete] if **every <Cauchy sequence> in it converges to a point of that same space**. A limit in a larger ambient completion is insufficient for this definition.

= c
{parent=2010/E/E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/E/E4/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/c/i}

**Yes.** A <Cauchy sequence> in the supremum <norm> converges in the given complete space $B([0,1])$ to a bounded function $f$. Supremum-<norm> convergence is <uniform convergence>. The uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous: for a point $x_0$ and tolerance $\epsilon$, choose a continuous $f_n$ within $\epsilon/3$ of $f$ uniformly, then use its <continuity> at $x_0$ and the <triangle inequality>. Hence $f\in C([0,1])$. Thus $C([0,1])$ is a closed, complete subspace of $B([0,1])$.

= ii
{parent=2010/E/E4/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/E/E4/c/ii}

**Yes.** <Continuity> and nonvanishing on the compact <interval> give constants
$$
0<m=\min_{[0,1]}|\alpha|\leq\max_{[0,1]}|\alpha|=M<\infty.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{m\|f-g\|_\infty\leq d_\alpha(f,g)\leq M\|f-g\|_\infty.}
$$
A $d_\alpha$-<Cauchy sequence> is therefore supremum-<norm> Cauchy and has a continuous uniform limit $f$ by part (i). The upper bound then makes it converge to $f$ also in $d_\alpha$. Equivalently, multiplication by $\alpha$ is an isometry from this weighted space onto ordinary $C([0,1])$, since division by the continuous nonvanishing $\alpha$ gives its inverse.

This is <completeness of a continuous weighted uniform norm>.

= F
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Multivariable Calculus}

= F2
{parent=2010/F}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/F/F2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/F/F2/a}

<Differentiability> at $x_0$ means that a <linear map> $A:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ exists with
$$
f(x_0+h)=f(x_0)+Ah+o(\|h\|).
$$
The map $A=Df(x_0)$ is the <total derivative>. A <linear map> between these finite-dimensional spaces is bounded, so $\|f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)\|\leq\|A\|\|h\|+o(\|h\|)\to0$. Thus <differentiability> implies <continuity>.

For the displayed two-variable function,
$$
0\leq\frac{x^4}{\sqrt{x^4+y^2}}\leq x^2\leq x^2+y^2.
$$
Writing $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ gives $|f(x,y)|/r\leq r\to0$. Therefore **it is differentiable at the origin, with derivative zero**.

Finally, put $h=x-x_0$, write $f(x_0+h)=Ah+r(h)$ with $r(h)=o(\|h\|)$, and $g(x_0+h)=g(x_0)+s(h)$ with $s(h)=o(1)$. Since $f(x_0)=0$,
$$
F(x_0+h)=\langle Ah,g(x_0)\rangle
+\langle Ah,s(h)\rangle+\langle r(h),g(x_0)+s(h)\rangle.
$$
By the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> the last two terms are $o(\|h\|)$: $A$ is bounded and $g(x_0)+s(h)$ stays bounded. Hence
$$
\boxed{DF(x_0)h=\langle Df(x_0)h,g(x_0)\rangle.}
$$
Only <continuity> of $g$ at $x_0$ is needed, because the vanishing of $f(x_0)$ removes the term which would otherwise require differentiating $g$.

= b
{parent=2010/F/F2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/F/F2/b}

A $C^1$ <embedded submanifold> $M\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ of dimension $n-1$ is locally the graph of a $C^1$ function of $n-1$ coordinates after a change of coordinates. Equivalently, near each point $x_0\in M$ it is a regular level set $\{x:h(x)=0\}$ of a $C^1$ scalar function with $\nabla h(x_0)\neq0$.

The <Lagrange multiplier> theorem says that if a $C^1$ function $F$ has a constrained local extremum at $x_0$ on such a level set, then
$$
\boxed{\nabla F(x_0)=\lambda\nabla h(x_0)}
$$
for some real $\lambda$. Indeed the derivative of $F$ must vanish on the <tangent space> $T_{x_0}M=\ker Dh(x_0)$, whose normal space is the line spanned by $\nabla h(x_0)$. The nonzero-gradient condition is essential.

= c
{parent=2010/F/F2}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=2010/F/F2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/F/F2/c/1}

Work on the positive orthant, where all real powers in $g$ are defined. To justify a global maximum, enlarge the constraint set to the compact simplex
$$
K=\{x_i\geq0:\sum_i p_ix_i=1\}.
$$
It is closed and bounded since $x_i\leq1/p_i$. The product $g$ extends continuously to $K$, is zero at every boundary point where a coordinate vanishes, and equals $1$ at $(1,\ldots,1)$. Hence a maximum exists and lies in the positive interior. For $n=1$ the constraint gives $x_1=1$ directly.

For $n\geq2$, at an interior maximum the <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
\partial_i g=\frac{p_ig}{x_i}=\lambda p_i.
$$
All $p_i$ and $g$ are positive, so all $x_i$ are equal. The constraint and $\sum_i p_i=1$ make their common value $1$. Thus the only possible maximizer has value $1$, proving
$$
\boxed{g(x)\leq1\quad\text{on }M,}
$$
with equality exactly when every $x_i=1$. The source writes $g$ on all of $\mathbb R^n$; for general noninteger exponents its relevant real-valued domain is the positive orthant used here.

= 2
{parent=2010/F/F2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/F/F2/c/2}

Let $S=\sum_i p_ia_i>0$ and set $x_i=a_i/S$. Then $x_i>0$ and $\sum_i p_ix_i=1$, so part (1) gives
$$
1\geq\prod_i x_i^{p_i}
=\frac{\prod_i a_i^{p_i}}{S^{\sum_i p_i}}
=\frac{\prod_i a_i^{p_i}}S.
$$
Therefore the <weighted arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> is
$$
\boxed{\prod_i a_i^{p_i}\leq\sum_i p_ia_i.}
$$
Equality holds exactly when all $a_i$ are equal, by the equality case in part (1).

= G
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= G2
{parent=2010/G}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/G/G2}

At a regular constrained extremal, the first variations satisfy $\delta I=\lambda\delta J$ for a constant <Lagrange multiplier> $\lambda$. One way to justify this is to choose a variation for which $\delta J\neq0$, use it to correct any other variation to preserve the constraint by the <implicit function theorem>, and differentiate $I$ along that corrected family. This gives the same ratio $\lambda$ for all variations. The exceptional case $\delta J=0$ for every variation requires the usual separate abnormal-extremal analysis.

Set $L=F-\lambda G$. Integration by parts in its <first variation> gives
$$
\delta(I-\lambda J)
=\left[L_{y'}\eta\right]_a^b+
\int_a^b\left(L_y-\frac d{dx}L_{y'}\right)\eta\,dx.
$$
Taking compactly supported variations and applying the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> yields the <Euler-Lagrange equation>. For the quadratic integrands, it is
$$
\boxed{(py')'+(q+\lambda r)y=0,}
$$
or $\mathcal Ly=\lambda ry$ with $\mathcal L y=-(py')'-qy$. If endpoint values are free, independent endpoint variations give the natural conditions
$$
\boxed{p(a)y'(a)=p(b)y'(b)=0.}
$$
At a prescribed endpoint the variation instead vanishes and no natural condition is added there.

Under these natural conditions, integration by parts gives
$$
2I=\int_a^b(py'^2-qy^2)\,dx
=[py'y]_a^b+\int_a^by\mathcal Ly\,dx
=\lambda\int_a^bry^2\,dx=2\lambda J.
$$
Hence $\boxed{I=\lambda J}$. For two solutions satisfying the same natural conditions, subtract their cross-multiplied equations and integrate:
$$
(\lambda_m-\lambda_n)\int_a^bry_my_n\,dx
=\int_a^b(y_n\mathcal Ly_m-y_m\mathcal Ly_n)\,dx=0.
$$
The final equality is the vanishing endpoint boundary form. Thus distinct <eigenvalue>[eigenvalues] give
$$
\boxed{\int_a^b r y_m y_n\,dx=0.}
$$
The same argument works for other common homogeneous boundary conditions making that boundary form zero.

In the Legendre case, $((1-x^2)y_n')'+\lambda_ny_n=0$. Comparing coefficients of $x^n$ in a degree-$n$ solution gives $\lambda_n=n(n+1)$, so different degrees have different <eigenvalue>[eigenvalues]. A degree-zero solution is $y_0=C_0$. A linear solution $Ax+B$ must be orthogonal to the nonzero constant solution, so $B=0$ and $y_1=C_1x$. Write $y_2=Ax^2+Bx+C$. <Orthogonality> to $x$ gives $B=0$, and <orthogonality> to $1$ gives $2A/3+2C=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y_2=C_2\frac{3x^2-1}{2}.}
$$
The constants are free <normalization> factors; for solutions of the stated exact degrees they are nonzero. The natural endpoint conditions hold automatically for these <polynomial>[polynomials] because $p(\pm1)=0$.

= H
{parent=2010}
{scope}
