{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= H2
{parent=2010/H}
{scope}
{c}

Oxford advertises worked solutions to this exact question for its students in https://users.ox.ac.uk/~math0391/CMrevision.pdf[the Classical Mechanics revision notes]. It is intentionally omitted from the newly authored solutions.

= J
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Quantum Theory}

= J2
{parent=2010/J}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/J/J2}

The stationary <Schrödinger equation> is
$$
-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\psi''(x)+V(x)\psi(x)=E\psi(x).
$$
A nonzero bound-state <wave function> has $\int_{\mathbb R}|\psi|^2dx<\infty$, so it can be rescaled to have integral one. Odd <parity> means $\psi(-x)=-\psi(x)$.

For $-V_0<E<0$, set $k=\sqrt{2m(V_0+E)}/\hbar>0$ and $\kappa=\sqrt{-2mE}/\hbar>0$. Oddness removes the interior cosine solution, and normalizability removes the growing exponentials outside. A continuous odd candidate can therefore be written
$$
\psi(x)=A\begin{cases}
-\sin(ka)e^{\kappa(x+a)},&x\leq-a,\\
\sin(kx),&|x|<a,\\
\sin(ka)e^{-\kappa(x-a)},&x\geq a.
\end{cases}
$$
<Continuity> of the derivative at $a$ gives $k\cos(ka)=-\kappa\sin(ka)$; oddness gives the other boundary condition. A nonzero solution cannot have $\sin(ka)=0$, since the derivative condition would then force its interior coefficient to vanish. Thus, with $\xi=ka$ and $\eta=\kappa a$,
$$
\boxed{\eta=-\xi\cot\xi,\qquad
\xi^2+\eta^2=\frac{2mV_0a^2}{\hbar^2}.}
$$
Physical <bound state>[bound states] require $\xi,\eta>0$. On $0<\xi<\pi/2$, the cotangent condition gives negative $\eta$, so no physical intersection occurs. On $(\pi/2,\pi)$, the curve $-\xi\cot\xi$ rises from zero to positive infinity, while the positive quadrant of the circle decreases. Their first intersection exists exactly when the circle radius $R=\sqrt{2mV_0a^2/\hbar^2}$ exceeds $\pi/2$.

\Image[2010-odd-well-bound-states.png]
{title=Odd-parity finite-well energies from a circle and a cotangent curve}
{description=Only positive eta gives a bound state. A circle of radius below pi over two misses the positive cotangent branch, while a larger circle intersects it.}

In particular,
$$
\boxed{2mV_0<\left(\frac{\pi\hbar}{2a}\right)^2
\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{no odd-parity bound state}.}
$$
There is also no normalizable odd state at equality: the putative intersection has $\eta=0$, hence $E=0$, and its exterior tail does not decay. Higher positive branches begin beyond this first <interval> and require still larger $R$.

= K
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics and Waves}

= K3
{parent=2010/K}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/K/K3}

The <Milne-Thomson circle theorem> says that if a complex potential $f(z)$ has no singularities on or inside $|z|=a$, then a circular impermeable boundary can be introduced by taking
$$
W(z)=f(z)+f^*(a^2/z),\qquad f^*(w)=\overline{f(\overline w)}.
$$
The new singularities are the reflected ones inside the circle. On $|z|=a$, $a^2/z=\overline z$, so $W(z)=f(z)+\overline{f(z)}$ is real; the <stream function> is constant and there is no normal velocity. At infinity the added term tends to $f^*(0)$, a harmless potential constant, so it preserves the far-<field> flow. This proves the theorem with zero added net <circulation> about the cylinder. A logarithmic term may then specify a different cylinder <circulation>.

For $f(z)=-i\Gamma\log(z-b)/(2\pi)$, put $c=a^2/b$. The reflected term is
$$
f^*(a^2/z)=\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(a^2/z-b)
=\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(z-c)-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log z+\text{constant},
$$
with locally consistent logarithm branches. The circle-theorem solution has zero cylinder <circulation>: its image <vortex> at $c$ and its central <vortex> cancel. To impose <circulation> $-\Gamma$, add $i\Gamma\log z/(2\pi)$. The result is
$$
\boxed{w(z)=-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(z-b)
+\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(z-c)+\text{constant}.}
$$
It consists of the physical <vortex> and an opposite image <vortex> inside the obstacle. On the circle, $|z-b|=(b/a)|z-c|$, so its <stream function> is indeed constant.

The <complex velocity> is
$$
q(z)=w'(z)=-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}
\left(\frac1{z-b}-\frac1{z-c}\right).
$$
Inside the positively oriented obstacle contour, $q^2$ has only the double pole at $c$. Its <residue> is
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{z=c}q(z)^2
=\left.\frac d{dz}\left[-\frac{\Gamma^2(b-c)^2}{4\pi^2(z-b)^2}\right]\right|_{z=c}
=-\frac{\Gamma^2}{2\pi^2(b-c)}.
$$
The <residue theorem> and the supplied <Blasius force theorem> therefore give
$$
F_x-iF_y=\frac{i\rho}{2}(2\pi i)\left[-\frac{\Gamma^2}{2\pi^2(b-c)}\right]
=\frac{\rho\Gamma^2}{2\pi(b-c)}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{F_x=\frac{\rho\Gamma^2b}{2\pi(b^2-a^2)},\qquad F_y=0.}
$$
The force points toward the held <vortex>, independent of the sign of its <circulation>.

The residue calculation is the <force on a circular cylinder from a held exterior vortex>.

= K4
{parent=2010/K}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2010/K/K4}

The <conformal map> $\zeta=z^3$ sends the wedge $0<\arg z<\pi/3$ to the upper half-plane. The physical <vortex> maps to $\zeta=c^3$; an opposite image <vortex> at $\overline c^{,3}$ makes the real axis a streamline. Pulling this potential back gives
$$
w(z)=-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(z^3-c^3)
+\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log(z^3-\overline c^{,3}).
$$
Since $z^3-c^3=(z-c)(z^2+cz+c^2)$, splitting the first logarithm, up to an additive constant, gives exactly the required form. The image construction enforces zero normal velocity on both rays.

To find the <vortex> motion, hold $c$ fixed while differentiating with respect to $z$, remove the self-induced singular term $-i\Gamma/(2\pi(z-c))$, and then evaluate at $z=c$. The remaining <complex velocity> is
$$
\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\left[-\frac{2c+c}{c^2+c^2+c^2}
+\frac{3c^2}{c^3-\overline c^{,3}}\right].
$$
A <complex velocity> $u-iv$ equals $d\overline c/dt$, rather than $dc/dt$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\overline c}{dt}=\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}
\left(-\frac1c+\frac{3c^2}{c^3-\overline c^{,3}}\right).}
$$
The overbar on the source derivative is essential to the trajectory calculation.

Write $c=re^{i\theta}$. Since $c^3-\overline c^{,3}=2ir^3\sin(3\theta)$, multiply the equation by $e^{i\theta}$ to obtain
$$
\dot r-ir\dot\theta
=\frac{3\Gamma}{4\pi r}\cot(3\theta)+\frac{i\Gamma}{4\pi r}.
$$
Taking real and imaginary parts gives
$$
\dot r=\frac{3\Gamma}{4\pi r}\cot(3\theta),\qquad
\dot\theta=-\frac{\Gamma}{4\pi r^2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\frac d{dt}\big(r\sin(3\theta)\big)
=\dot r\sin(3\theta)+3r\cos(3\theta)\dot\theta=0,
$$
proving the trajectory invariant
$$
\boxed{r\sin(3\theta)=\text{constant}.}
$$
The calculation also covers $\Gamma=0$, when the <vortex> position is stationary.

This is the sixty-degree case of a <point vortex in a wedge>.

= M
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probability}

= M4
{parent=2010/M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/M/M4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/M/M4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M4/a/i}

Away from the null event $X=0$, the inverse transformation is $x=u$, $y=uv$. Its <Jacobian determinant> is $u$, so its absolute value is $|u|$. <Independence> therefore gives the joint density
$$
\boxed{f_{U,V}(u,v)=|u|f(u)g(uv).}
$$
A value at $u=0$ may be assigned arbitrarily without changing the distribution.

= ii
{parent=2010/M/M4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M4/a/ii}

Integrate the joint density over $u$:
$$
f_V(v)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|u|f(u)g(uv)\,du.
$$
Since both original densities are even, substitution $u\mapsto-u$ shows that the negative and positive halves contribute equally. Hence
$$
\boxed{f_V(v)=2\int_0^\infty u f(u)g(uv)\,du.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2010/M/M4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M4/a/iii}

For positive standard deviations $a,b$, substitute the two normal densities:
$$
f_V(v)=\frac1{\pi ab}\int_0^\infty u\exp\left[-\frac{u^2}{2}\left(a^{-2}+v^2b^{-2}\right)\right]du.
$$
The integral is $1/(a^{-2}+v^2b^{-2})$, by substituting the exponent's quadratic argument. Thus, with $c=b/a$,
$$
\boxed{f_V(v)=\frac{ab}{\pi(b^2+a^2v^2)}=\frac c{\pi(c^2+v^2)}.}
$$
This is a centered <Cauchy distribution> of scale $c$. Since $P(X=0)=0$, the desired probability is
$$
\boxed{P(|Y|<|X|)=P(|V|<1)
=\frac2\pi\arctan\frac1c=\frac2\pi\arctan\frac ab.}
$$

= b
{parent=2010/M/M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M4/b}

Take $U=Y$ and $V=XY^2$. The inverse is $y=u$, $x=v/u^2$, with absolute Jacobian $u^{-2}$. The original unit-square support becomes $0<u<1$ and $0<v<u^2$, so the joint density is $u^{-2}$ there. For a fixed $0<v<1$, the admissible range is $\sqrt v<u<1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f_V(v)=\begin{cases}v^{-1/2}-1,&0<v<1,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
It is nonnegative and integrates to $\int_0^1(v^{-1/2}-1)dv=1$, as required.

= M5
{parent=2010/M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/M/M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/a/i}

A time-homogeneous <Markov chain> on a countable <state space> $S$ is a sequence of $S$-valued <random variable>[random variables] for which, for every positive-probability finite history,
$$
P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_0=i_0,\ldots,X_n=i)
=P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i)=P_{ij}.
$$
Thus the conditional next-step law depends only on the current state, and the transition probabilities do not depend on time.

= ii
{parent=2010/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/a/ii}

The <transition matrix> is $P=(P_{ij})_{i,j\in S}$, where $P_{ij}=P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i)$. Its entries are nonnegative and each row sums to one. On states absent from a particular initial law, these probabilities specify the same chain when started from that state.

= b
{parent=2010/M/M5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/b}

For $n=0$, the transition probabilities are the entries of the identity $P^0$. Suppose the formula holds for $n$. Conditioning on $X_n=k$ and using the <Markov property> gives
$$
P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_0=i)
=\sum_{k\in S}P(X_n=k\mid X_0=i)P_{kj}
=\sum_k(P^n)_{ik}P_{kj}=(P^{n+1})_{ij}.
$$
The countable sums have nonnegative terms, so they are justified by the <law of total probability>. Induction proves
$$
\boxed{P(X_n=j\mid X_0=i)=(P^n)_{ij}.}
$$

= c
{parent=2010/M/M5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/c}

Conditioning on the initial state gives
$$
\boxed{P(X_n=i)=\sum_{j\in S}\lambda_j(P^n)_{ji}=(\lambda P^n)_i.}
$$
Thus the row-vector distribution at time $n$ is $\lambda P^n$.

= d
{parent=2010/M/M5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/d}

If $\lambda P=\lambda$, induction gives $\lambda P^n=\lambda$ for every $n\geq0$. Part (c) then makes the distribution of every $X_n$ equal to $\lambda$. Such a probability row vector is a <stationary distribution>.

= e
{parent=2010/M/M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/M/M5/e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/e/i}

For $i\geq1$, the stationarity equation is
$$
\pi_i=a\pi_{i-1}+(1-a-b)\pi_i+b\pi_{i+1},
$$
which rearranges to the stated recurrence. At zero,
$$
\pi_0=(1-a)\pi_0+b\pi_1,
\qquad\boxed{b\pi_1=a\pi_0.}
$$
Define the adjacent probability current $J_i=a\pi_i-b\pi_{i+1}$. The recurrence says $J_i=J_{i-1}$, and the boundary equation says $J_0=0$. Hence $\pi_{i+1}=(a/b)\pi_i$ for all $i$. Since $\rho=a/b<1$, <normalization> gives
$$
\boxed{\pi_i=(1-\rho)\rho^i,\qquad\rho=\frac ab,\quad i\geq0.}
$$
The adjacent balance equations also verify stationarity directly. If $a=0$, this formula means the point mass at zero; otherwise the <birth-death chain> is irreducible and this is its unique stationary <probability distribution>.

= ii
{parent=2010/M/M5/e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/M/M5/e/ii}

Let $T_0^+=\inf\{n\geq1:X_n=0\}$. For $a>0$, the chain is irreducible and its stationary <probability distribution> makes it positive recurrent. The mean-return-time identity gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_0T_0^+=\frac1{\pi_0}=\frac b{b-a}.}
$$
This counts a self-loop at zero as a return after one time unit, as “next zero” requires. Equivalently, with probability $1-a$ that immediate return occurs; with probability $a$ the chain moves to one and then has mean hitting time $1/(b-a)$ back to zero, giving $1+a/(b-a)$. If $a=0$, the return is deterministically at time one and the displayed formula still equals one.

= O
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= O4
{parent=2010/O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/O/O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O4/a}

The <maximum likelihood estimation>[maximum-likelihood estimator] is a maximizer
$$
\widehat\theta\in\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\theta}\ L_n(\theta),
\qquad L_n(\theta)=\prod_{i=1}^nf(X_i;\theta).
$$
Writing $\ell_n=\log L_n$ and the score $S_n=\nabla_\theta\ell_n$, the expected <Fisher information> <matrix> is
$$
\mathcal I_n(\theta)=\mathbb E_\theta[S_nS_n^T]
=-\mathbb E_\theta[\nabla_\theta^2\ell_n],
$$
where the equality uses the usual regularity conditions. For independent identically distributed observations, $\mathcal I_n=n\mathcal I_1$.

Under the standard identifiability, interior-parameter, <differentiability> and nonsingular-information assumptions, the large-sample distribution is
$$
\boxed{\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)\xrightarrow d
N(0,\mathcal I_1(\theta)^{-1}),}
$$
or approximately $\widehat\theta\sim N(\theta,\mathcal I_n(\theta)^{-1})$. Boundary parameters need not obey this regular approximation.

= b
{parent=2010/O/O4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/O/O4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O4/b/i}

The two totals are independent, with $n\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2N,q)$ and $m\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2M,p)$. Their joint likelihood is
$$
L(p,q)=\binom{2N}nq^n(1-q)^{2N-n}
\binom{2M}mp^m(1-p)^{2M-m}.
$$
Differentiating the separated <log-likelihood> gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat p=\frac m{2M},\qquad\widehat q=\frac n{2N}.}
$$
These formulas include all-zero or all-one totals as boundary maxima. For interior $p,q$, the <Fisher information> in the ordered parameter vector $(p,q)$ is
$$
\mathcal I(p,q)=\begin{pmatrix}
\dfrac{2M}{p(1-p)}&0\\[2mm]
0&\dfrac{2N}{q(1-q)}
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The off-diagonal entries vanish because the likelihood separates, and the two estimates are independent even at finite sample size. For large $M,N$, their joint distribution is approximately
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}\widehat p\\\widehat q\end{pmatrix}
\sim N\left(\begin{pmatrix}p\\q\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}p(1-p)/(2M)&0\\0&q(1-q)/(2N)\end{pmatrix}\right).}
$$

= ii
{parent=2010/O/O4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O4/b/ii}

A linear combination of independent normal variables is normal; the corresponding asymptotic statement follows from the joint <central limit theorem> and the <continuous mapping theorem>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat p-\widehat q\ \approx\ N\left(p-q,
\frac{p(1-p)}{2M}+\frac{q(1-q)}{2N}\right).}
$$
The two variances add because of <independence>. This approximation assumes both sample sizes grow and the true probabilities are interior.

= iii
{parent=2010/O/O4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O4/b/iii}

Under the null, replace both parameters by a common $r$. The likelihood becomes
$$
L_0(r)=\binom{2N}n\binom{2M}m
r^{n+m}(1-r)^{2(N+M)-n-m}.
$$
The pooled maximum-likelihood estimate is
$$
\boxed{\widehat r=\frac{n+m}{2(N+M)}.}
$$
Under an interior null $p=q=r$, part (ii) gives a centered asymptotic normal difference with <variance> $r(1-r)(1/(2M)+1/(2N))$. The <law of large numbers> makes $\widehat r\to r$ in probability. By Slutsky's theorem, replacing $r$ by this consistent estimate gives
$$
\boxed{Z=\frac{\widehat p-\widehat q}
{\sqrt{(1/(2M)+1/(2N))\widehat r(1-\widehat r)}}\xrightarrow dN(0,1).}
$$
For the two-sided alternative, reject when $|Z|>z_{1-\alpha/2}$, the corresponding standard-normal <quantile>. This has asymptotic <significance level> $\alpha$.

= iv
{parent=2010/O/O4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O4/b/iv}

For a two-sided 5% test, the critical values are $\pm1.96$. Since $|-1.89|<1.96$, **do not reject the null at the 5% level**.

For the prespecified directional alternative $q>p$, the numerator $\widehat p-\widehat q$ tends to be negative. Use the lower-tail critical region $Z<-1.64$ at the 5% level. Since $-1.89<-1.64$, **reject in favor of $q>p$ in the one-sided test**. The direction should be chosen from the additional scientific information rather than selected after inspecting the sign of this same statistic.

= O5
{parent=2010/O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O5/a}

The <significance level> of a test is a bound $\alpha$ on its probability of rejecting a true null; for a composite null the bound is uniform over its parameter values. Its power at an alternative parameter is its rejection probability under that parameter.

The <Neyman-Pearson lemma> concerns a simple null density $f_0$ and a simple alternative density $f_1$ for the whole sample. Among tests of size at most $\alpha$, a most powerful test rejects where $f_1>kf_0$, accepts where $f_1<kf_0$, and if necessary randomizes on equality so that its size is $\alpha$. Let its rejection probability function be $\phi_*$, with $0\leq\phi_*\leq1$, and let $\phi$ be any competing test of size at most $\alpha$.

Pointwise,
$$
(\phi_* -\phi)(f_1-kf_0)\geq0:
$$
on the rejection side both factors are nonnegative, and on the acceptance side both are nonpositive. Integrating gives
$$
\int\phi_*f_1-\int\phi f_1
\geq k\left(\int\phi_*f_0-\int\phi f_0\right)\geq0.
$$
Thus the likelihood-ratio test has at least as much power as any competitor. Even when the sample has a continuous density, the <likelihood ratio> can have an atom, so boundary randomization may still be necessary in the general statement. For the exponential test below the statistic has a continuous distribution and no randomization is needed.

= b
{parent=2010/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/O/O5/b}

For a fixed alternative $0<\lambda<\lambda_0$, the joint <likelihood ratio> is
$$
\frac{L(\lambda)}{L(\lambda_0)}
=\left(\frac\lambda{\lambda_0}\right)^n
\exp\left((\lambda_0-\lambda)\sum_i x_i\right).
$$
It is strictly increasing in $S=\sum_i x_i$. By the <Neyman-Pearson lemma>, the most powerful level-$\alpha$ rejection region is therefore $S\geq c_\alpha$. Under the null, $S\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(n,\lambda_0)$ with the second parameter interpreted as a rate. Its continuous distribution gives
$$
\boxed{c_\alpha=q(1-\alpha;n,\lambda_0).}
$$
This threshold depends on the null and $\alpha$, but not on the particular smaller alternative $\lambda$. Thus the same test is most powerful for every $0<\lambda<\lambda_0$, making it <uniformly most powerful test>[uniformly most powerful].

Under a true alternative $\lambda$, $S\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(n,\lambda)$, so its attained power is
$$
\boxed{Q(\lambda)=1-F(q(1-\alpha;n,\lambda_0);n,\lambda).}
$$
For each $\lambda<\lambda_0$, the <Neyman-Pearson lemma> bounds the power of every other level-$\alpha$ test by this value. Hence the displayed function is not only an upper bound but the attainable power envelope on the specified alternative.

= P
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= P3
{parent=2010/P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/a}

A best approximation to $x$ from $U_n$ is a vector $u_*\in U_n$ such that
$$
\boxed{\|x-u_*\|=\inf_{u\in U_n}\|x-u\|.}
$$
The definition requires attainment of the infimum. This finite-dimensional subspace does have such an approximation, as the explicit projection below shows; <completeness> of the whole inner-product space is unnecessary.

= b
{parent=2010/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/b}

Expand both squared norms using the <inner product>:
$$
\|x\pm y\|^2=\|x\|^2+\|y\|^2\pm2\operatorname{Re}\langle x,y\rangle.
$$
Adding cancels the cross terms, giving the <parallelogram law>
$$
\boxed{\|x-y\|^2+\|x+y\|^2=2(\|x\|^2+\|y\|^2).}
$$
The real part may be omitted in a real inner-product space.

= c
{parent=2010/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/c}

Suppose $u,v\in U_n$ both minimize the distance, with common value $d$. Apply the parallelogram identity to $x-u$ and $x-v$:
$$
4\left\|x-\frac{u+v}2\right\|^2+\|u-v\|^2=4d^2.
$$
The midpoint belongs to the linear subspace $U_n$, so its distance from $x$ is at least $d$. It follows that $\|u-v\|^2\leq0$, hence $u=v$. Thus **the best approximation is unique**.

= d
{parent=2010/P/P3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2010/P/P3/d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/d/i}

Let $v=\sum_{k=1}^n\langle x,e_k\rangle e_k$, using the convention that the <inner product> is linear in its first argument. Orthonormality gives
$$
\langle x-v,e_j\rangle=\langle x,e_j\rangle-\sum_k\langle x,e_k\rangle\langle e_k,e_j\rangle=0
$$
for every $j$. Hence $x-v$ is orthogonal to $U_n$. For any $u\in U_n$, the Pythagorean identity gives $\|x-u\|^2=\|x-v\|^2+\|v-u\|^2$, so $v$ is the unique best approximation. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P_nx=\sum_{k=1}^n\langle x,e_k\rangle e_k.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2010/P/P3/d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/d/ii}

Expand the <norm> of the orthonormal-<basis> expression. All cross terms vanish and $\|e_k\|=1$, so
$$
\boxed{\|P_nx\|^2=\sum_{k=1}^n|\langle x,e_k\rangle|^2.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2010/P/P3/d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P3/d/iii}

The residual $x-P_nx$ is orthogonal to $P_nx\in U_n$. Thus the <Pythagorean theorem> gives $\|x\|^2=\|x-P_nx\|^2+\|P_nx\|^2$. Substitute part (ii) and rearrange:
$$
\boxed{\|x-P_nx\|^2=\|x\|^2-\sum_{k=1}^n|\langle x,e_k\rangle|^2.}
$$
Nonnegativity of the left-hand side also gives the finite form of <Bessel's inequality>.

= P4
{parent=2010/P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P4/a}

Assume the usual nondegenerate <interval> $a<b$. The integral is bilinear and symmetric because the functions and weight are real. It is nonnegative on $(f,f)$. If $f$ is nonzero at a point, <continuity> makes $|f|$ bounded below by a positive number on a nontrivial relative <interval> nearby, and the positive continuous weight is also bounded below there. Hence $\int_a^bf^2w>0$. Thus the formula is positive definite and defines an <inner product>.

The functions are orthogonal precisely when
$$
\boxed{\langle f,g\rangle=\int_a^bf(x)g(x)w(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
A singleton <interval> would make this integral zero for every function, so the condition $a<b$ is necessary.

= b
{parent=2010/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P4/b}

The intended orthogonal-<polynomial> convention is **$\deg p_k=k$**. An arbitrary <orthonormal basis> without this degree ordering does not satisfy the claim: permuting a standard <basis> can place the nonzero constant <polynomial> last, where it has no <root>[roots] rather than $n+1$. The following proof uses the required degree ordering.

It makes $p_k$ orthogonal to every <polynomial> of degree less than $k$, since $p_0,\ldots,p_{k-1}$ form a <basis> of that lower-degree space. Let $c_1,\ldots,c_r$ be the distinct <root>[roots] in $(a,b)$ at which $p_k$ changes sign, and put $q(x)=\prod_{j=1}^r(x-c_j)$, with $q=1$ if $r=0$. The product $p_kq$ has constant sign on $(a,b)$ except at its finitely many zeros: each sign change of $p_k$ is canceled by that of $q$.

If $r<k$, then $q$ has degree below $k$, so <orthogonality> gives $\int_a^bp_kqw=0$. But a nonzero constant-sign continuous product against the positive weight has a nonzero integral, a contradiction. Thus $r\geq k$. Since $\deg p_k=k$, there are exactly $k$ such <root>[roots], all simple, with no additional endpoint <root>[roots]. In particular
$$
\boxed{p_k\text{ has exactly }k\text{ distinct roots, all in }(a,b).}
$$
This also includes $k=0$, where the <polynomial> is nonzero constant and has no <root>[roots].

= c
{parent=2010/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/P/P4/c}

Use the intended degree ordering from part (b). Let $\ell_j$ be the degree-$n$ Lagrange cardinal <polynomial> at the distinct <root>[roots] $x_0,\ldots,x_n$ of $p_{n+1}$:
$$
\ell_j(x)=\prod_{i\neq j}\frac{x-x_i}{x_j-x_i},\qquad
w_j=\int_a^b\ell_j(x)w(x)\,dx.
$$
For any $f\in\Pi_{2n+1}$, <polynomial division> gives $f=sp_{n+1}+r$ with $\deg s\leq n$ and $\deg r\leq n$. <Orthogonality> of $p_{n+1}$ to $\Pi_n$ makes the integral of $sp_{n+1}$ zero, and that product also vanishes at every quadrature node. Meanwhile $r=\sum_jr(x_j)\ell_j$ by interpolation. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\int_a^bf(x)w(x)\,dx=\int_a^br(x)w(x)\,dx
=\sum_{j=0}^nw_jf(x_j).}
$$
Thus the rule is exact up to degree $2n+1$. To determine the sign of a weight, apply exactness to $\ell_j^2$, of degree $2n$:
$$
\boxed{w_j=\sum_iw_i\ell_j(x_i)^2
=\int_a^b\ell_j(x)^2w(x)\,dx>0.}
$$
The inequality is strict because $\ell_j$ is a nonzero <polynomial> and the weight is positive. This is the <Gaussian quadrature> construction; the source asks only for nonnegative weights, and the proof gives positivity.
